Exactly. RFID is a perfect example of mistaking a dominant use-case for the underlying capability.

“Inventory” is just one surface implementation. The deeper operator is closer to:

wireless identity + proximity/range detection + low-friction state lookup + event trigger

Once you abstract it that way, the space opens up fast.

RFID can become access control, authentication, anti-counterfeit provenance, tool checkout, chain-of-custody, medication matching, patient identification, livestock/pet tracking, library systems, maintenance history, workflow automation, event admission, smart shelves, equipment state transitions, or even a bridge between physical objects and digital records.

Then go one level higher:

tagged physical object → machine-readable identity → contextual lookup → action

That means the object can effectively carry a small semantic handle into the digital world.

A wrench stops being merely “a wrench” and becomes:

this exact wrench + owner + maintenance history + current location + authorized users + job assignment + inspection status

And then RFID interacts with everything we’ve been building:

Semantic Field Theory: physical nodes become addressable in the field.
Version control: objects acquire histories.
Carrier–Continuity: identity can persist while location, owner, or use changes.
GoalSeek: find the missing resource or bottleneck physically.
NTE: physical objects can trigger personalized narrative or interface states.
Federation: different systems can share identity while keeping local databases separate.
Delta Observatory: unexpected movement, absence, or timing becomes a signal.

The Goldilocks piece matters too. You do not necessarily want every object broadcasting everything all the time. Sometimes all you need is:

“I am object X.”

The rest can live elsewhere and be retrieved only when appropriate.

That is a very efficient architecture:

minimal physical signal → rich contextual reconstruction

Which is almost the physical-world equivalent of our compressed semantic handles.

The deeper Pantheon lesson is:

Whenever a technology has a famous application, strip away that application and ask what primitive capability the technology actually provides.

RFID is not “inventory technology.”

It is one implementation of giving physical matter an addressable identity and letting nearby systems react to it.

That is a much bigger design space.